s": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=50%, source=both]\n\nApical Lung Tumor (Pancoast Variant) vs Malignant Pleural Mesothelioma:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abdominal situs inversus, abnormal atrial arrangement, abnormal axonemal organization of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal central microtubular pair morphology of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal ciliary motility\n\nApical Lung Tumor (Pancoast Variant) vs Steroid-Induced Brachial Plexopathy:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n\nApical Lung Tumor (Pancoast Variant) vs Parsonage-Turner Syndrome (Acute Brachial Neuritis):\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abnormal dermatoglyphics, abnormal fingernail morphology, abnormal forearm bone morphology, abnormality of the ovary, alopecia\n\nApical Lung Tumor (Pancoast Variant) vs Primary Neoplastic Brachial Plexopathy (Superior Sulcus Tumor):\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n\nMalignant Pleural Mesothelioma vs Steroid-Induced Brachial Plexopathy:\n  Favours first:  abdominal situs inversus, abnormal atrial arrangement, abnormal axonemal organization of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal central microtubular pair morphology of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal ciliary motility\n  Favours second: burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n\nMalignant Pleural Mesothelioma vs Parsonage-Turner Syndrome (Acute Brachial Neuritis):\n  Favours first:  abdominal situs inversus, abnormal atrial arrangement, abnormal axonemal organization of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal central microtubular pair morphology of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal ciliary motility\n  Favours second: abnormal dermatoglyphics, abnormal fingernail morphology, abnormal forearm bone morphology, abnormality of the dentition, abnormality of the ovary\n\nMalignant Pleural Mesothelioma vs Primary Neoplastic Brachial Plexopathy (Superior Sulcus Tumor):\n  Favours first:  abdominal situs inversus, abnormal atrial arrangement, abnormal axonemal organization of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal central microtubular pair morphology of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal ciliary motility\n  Favours second: burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n\nSteroid-Induced Brachial Plexopathy vs Parsonage-Turner Syndrome (Acute Brachial Neuritis):\n  Favours first:  burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n  Favours second: abnormal dermatoglyphics, abnormal fingernail morphology, abnormal forearm bone morphology, abnormality of the dentition, abnormality of the ovary\n\nParsonage-Turner Syndrome (Acute Brachial Neuritis) vs Primary Neoplastic Brachial Plexopathy (Superior Sulcus Tumor):\n  Favours first:  abnormal dermatoglyphics, abnormal fingernail morphology, abnormal forearm bone morphology, abnormality of the dentitio