ic disorder: considered but Down syndrome features suggest isolated anatomic issues more likely"], "alarm_features": ["Down syndrome facial dysmorphology (increases risk of Hirschsprung's disease)", "Neonatal intestinal failure (constipation, abdominal distension)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Anatomic GI Obstruction in Neonate", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.2644628099173554, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["Digital rectal exam yielding stool/flatulence weakly opposes duodenal atresia as a proximal obstruction would generally prevent distal meconium passage without requiring surgical decompression, though variants with communicating fistula may exhibit this finding."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B1.4"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is there evidence of bowel atresia on abdominal imaging?", "Did the physical exam suggest other anatomical anomalies?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Upper gastrointestinal contrast imaging", "Abdominal ultrasound to assess for malrotation or duodenal atresia"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Congenital Enterocolic Dysmotility Disorders", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.44628099173553715, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Down syndrome features (upslanting palpebral fissures, single transverse palmar crease) moderately support Hirschsprung disease due to the 10-20% increased incidence of HSCR in Down syndrome compared to general population, though the rectal exam showing stool evacuation is atypical yet not pathognomonic for exclusion."], "evidence_against": ["In classic Hirschsprung disease with distal colonic aganglionosis, spontaneous rectal evacuability is expected. The observed rectal patency represents a weak against if we assume proximal aganglionosis, but distal rectal ganglion cells may still exist in the absence of complete involvement."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is there a history of delayed meconium passage?", "Do rectal exam findings suggest aganglionosis (e.g., lack of stool/flatulence)?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Rectal biopsy for absence of ganglion cells", "Neonatal Hirschsprung's disease testing"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Neonatal Inborn Errors with GI Obstruction", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.08264462809917354, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Negative family history (2 healthy siblings) moderately weakens inborn errors like cystic fibrosis, as autosomal recessive conditions would typically exhibit recurrence risks not observed here, though de novo mutations cannot be entirely excluded."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Uncharacterized GI Obstruction (Imaging-dependent Finding in Figure A)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.2, "posterior": 0.16528925619834708, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["What specific radiographic patterns were seen in Figure A?", "Is there evidence of air-fluid levels or 'string sign'?"], "requestable_discriminators": [], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "inte