gh the colon?", "result_summary": "Digital rectal exam yielding stool/flatulence weakly opposes duodenal atresia as a proximal obstruction would generally prevent distal meconium passage without requiring surgical decompression, though variants with communicating fistula may exhibit this finding.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "weak_against", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "neutral", "B4": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 5, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the evacuation of stool and flatulence via digital rectal exam inconsistent with Hirschsprung disease’s hallmark finding of aganglionosis in the distal colon (implying an anatomically obstructed tract)?", "result_summary": "In classic Hirschsprung disease with distal colonic aganglionosis, spontaneous rectal evacuability is expected. The observed rectal patency represents a weak against if we assume proximal aganglionosis, but distal rectal ganglion cells may still exist in the absence of complete involvement.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2": "weak_against", "B3": "neutral", "B4": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 4, "bundle_size": 5, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the maternal refusal of prenatal testing and the presence of healthy older siblings weaken the likelihood of an inborn error (like cystic fibrosis) as the cause of intestinal failure in this neonate?", "result_summary": "Negative family history (2 healthy siblings) moderately weakens inborn errors like cystic fibrosis, as autosomal recessive conditions would typically exhibit recurrence risks not observed here, though de novo mutations cannot be entirely excluded.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "moderate_against", "B4": "neutral"}}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.2644628099173554, "B2": 0.4462809