 vomiting", "Bright red blood per rectum despite minimal systemic symptoms"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Acute Upper Gastrointestinal Injury with Hemorrhage", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.504, "posterior": 0.389542767261485, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Classic Mallory-Weiss syndrome presentation: forceful emesis following heavy alcohol intake. Vomiting-induced gastric mucosal injury is a well-recognized cause of upper GI bleeding that can manifest as hematochezia when blood traverses the GI tract quickly."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of forceful vomiting or alcohol-induced gastric bleeding", "Signs of acute upper GI bleeding (e.g., hematemesis, chest pain)"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Upper endoscopy for esophageal/gastric pathology", "Abdominal CT without contrast for vascular injuries"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Colonic Vascular Lesions with Hemorrhage", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.022780278787221345, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Young age without cardiac valve disease significantly reduces the pre-test probability of colonic vascular malformations."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Anorectal Disorders with Hematochezia", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.36000000000000004, "posterior": 0.15864837012529154, "danger": 0.1, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The absence of sphincter tenderness makes anal fissures unlikely but does not eliminate internal hemorrhoids as an etiology. Bright red rectal blood remains consistent with lower GI/ anorectal bleeding despite normal physical exam."], "evidence_against": ["Lack of anal findings makes external hemorrhoids and fissures unlikely, but internal hemorrhoids remain plausible as they typically don't cause anal mass or surface tenderness. This weakly contradicts anorectal pathologic findings."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B3.1", "B3.2"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Anal sphincter tenderness on exam", "History of hemorrhoidal swelling or thrombosis"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Digital rectal exam for external hemorrhoids", "Anoscopy for internal hemorrhoids/fissures"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.2, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "GI Malignancy with Lower Gastrointestinal Bleeding", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.018983565656017786, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Mallory-Weiss Syndrome with Esophagogastric Junction Tear", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.3690405163529858, "danger": 0.85, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The subacute bleeding pattern slightly weakens the likelihood of Mallory-Weiss syndrome, which usually presents with acute upper GI bleeds."]}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Boerhaave Syndrome with Esophageal Rupture", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.013668167272332808, "danger": 0.95, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The subacute bleeding pattern slightly weakens the likelihood of Mallory-Weiss syndrome, which usually presents with acute upper GI bleeds."]}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Acute Gastric Vascular Injury from Traumatic Erosion", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.006834083636166404, "danger": 0.75, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B3.1": {"id": "B3.1", "label": "Internal Hemorrhoids with Prolapse", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.14644464934642296, "danger": 0.1, "parent": "B3", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Alcohol ingestion without anal pressure or prior hemorrhoid symptoms slightly lowers the suspicion for hemorrhoids as the source."]}, "B3.2": {"id": "B3.2", "label": "Acute Anal Fissure with Superficial Tear", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.012203720778868579, "danger": 0.1, "parent": "B3", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absent anal pain and normal exam findings strongly argue against anal fissure, which is characteristically associated with severe defecation-related pain."]}}, "frontier": [], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 5, "bundle_size": 7, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the history of recent excessive alcohol consumption without prior GI symptoms or anal pressure suggest alternatives to hemorrhoids?", "result_summary": "Alcohol ingestion without anal pressure or prior hemorrhoid symptoms slightly lowers the suspicion for hemorrhoids as the source.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.1": "neutral", "B1.2": "neutral", "B1.3": "neutral", "B2": "neutral", "B3.1": "weak_against", "B3.2": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 6, "bundle_size": 7, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the complete absence of anal pain during/after defecation and normal rectal examination findings rule out anal fissure as a cause?", "result_summary": "Absent anal pain and normal exam findings strongly argue against anal fissure, which is characteristically associated with severe defecation-related pain.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.1": "neutral", "B1.2": "neutra